body:
# PR review

The controlled-index design is sound and fixes #4657 the right way (registering controlled items instead of bypassing the list), and I measured no perf cost — local 500-item typing benchmarks are within noise of master, with the narrowing scenario actually dropping from 17 to 15 renders. However, the rewrite moved the flush trigger from `register`/`unregister` (which used to schedule state) into `CompositeList`'s own layout effect, and that effect only runs when `CompositeList` itself re-renders — I confirmed a merge-blocking regression with a failing repro: items mounted by a state update in a deep subtree are never indexed.

## Bugs (2)

### 1. 🔴 Items mounted from a deep-subtree state update are never registered into the list

**Location:** `packages/react/src/internals/composite/list/CompositeList.tsx:41`

```ts
const register = useStableCallback((node: Element, metadata: CompositeMetadata<Metadata>) => {
  // ...
  map.set(node, metadata);
  isDirtyRef.current = true;
});
```

On master, `register`/`unregister` call `setMapTick`, so any item mounting anywhere re-renders `CompositeList` and re-syncs. In this PR they only set `isDirtyRef`, and the flush lives in a layout effect that runs **only when `CompositeList` re-renders**. The MutationObserver can't cover it either: additions produce `diff.size !== 0`, which deliberately skips `flush()` on the assumption that registration handles adds/removes. So a commit that mounts or unmounts an item **without re-rendering `CompositeList`** — local state in a subcomponent inside the popup, a memoized subtree — leaves `elementsRef` stale and the new item at index `-1` (its immediate `subscribeMapChange` snapshot predates the registration) until an unrelated re-render happens to flush.

**Failure scenario:** confirmed with a repro test that passes on master and fails on this branch:

```tsx
function DeepSection() {
  const [extra, setExtra] = React.useState(false);
  addItem = () => setExtra(true);
  return extra ? <Item label="extra" /> : null;
}

await render(
  <CompositeList elementsRef={elementsRef}>
    <Item label="a" />
    <DeepSection />
    <Item label="b" />
  </CompositeList>,
);

act(() => addItem!());
expect(elementsRef.current).toHaveLength(3); // PR: still 2; item "extra" keeps index -1
```

In product terms: a menu section that expands via its own state (`{open && <Menu.Item/>}`) mounts items that keyboard navigation, typeahead, and hover resolution all skip.

**Fix:** keep the new flush pipeline but restore a wake-up from registration — e.g. `setMapTick((t) => t + 1)` in `register`/`unregister` (React batches it per commit, and the dirty-check effect still coalesces the work), or a `queueMicrotask` fallback that flushes when `isDirtyRef` is still set after the commit. Please also add the repro above as a regression test.

### 2. ℹ️ `label: null` now falls through to `textContent`, losing the typeahead-exclusion semantic

**Location:** `packages/react/src/internals/composite/list/CompositeList.tsx` (syncRefs)

Old code wrote labels with `label !== undefined ? label : (textRef?.current?.textContent ?? node.textContent)`, so an explicit `label={null}` stored `null` — which `useTypeahead` treats as "skip this item". The new `metadata.label ?? metadata.textRef?.current?.textContent ?? node.textContent ?? null` coalesces `null` away and substitutes the text content. No first-party component passes `null` today (public `label` props are `string | undefined`), but `useCompositeListItem`'s param type is `string | null | undefined` and the hook is exposed via `/internals`, so the exclusion semantic is silently lost for anyone using it. Preserving the `!== undefined` check keeps both behaviors.

## Tests (1)

### 1. 🟡 The second mixed-index warning assertion is vacuous — `error()` dedupes by message

**Location:** `packages/react/src/internals/composite/list/CompositeList.test.tsx`

```tsx
await render(/* controlled first */);
expect(console.error).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expectedMessage);

await render(/* uncontrolled first */);
expect(console.error).toHaveBeenCalledWith(expectedMessage);
```

`error()` from `@base-ui/utils/error` deduplicates by message string, and the test setup only calls its `reset()` **between tests** (`test/setupVitest.ts`). Within this single test, the second render's warning is swallowed by the dedup set, and the second `toHaveBeenCalledWith` passes via the *first* call — the uncontrolled-then-controlled ordering is never actually verified. Splitting into two tests (so `reset()` runs in between) or asserting `toHaveBeenCalledTimes` makes the coverage real.

## Simplifications (1)

### 1. 🟡 `ComboboxItem`'s parallel `listRef` bookkeeping is now redundant for non-virtualized controlled items

**Location:** `packages/react/src/combobox/item/ComboboxItem.tsx:84`

```ts
const shouldRun = hasRegistered && (virtualized || indexProp != null);
// ...
list[index] = itemRef.current;
```

This effect was the registration mechanism for controlled items back when they bypassed `CompositeList` — the wipe-out interaction between it and `CompositeList`'s length-truncation is the actual root cause of #4657. With this PR, non-virtualized controlled items register normally and `syncRefs` rebuilds `elementsRef` wholesale (replacing the array identity, which also makes this effect's cleanup operate on a dead array). The `indexProp != null` arm can be dropped, leaving `virtualized` only (where `CompositeList` isn't rendered and the effect is still load-bearing). Two writers for the same slot is exactly the kind of coupling that made the original bug hard to see.

## Docs (1)

### 1. 🟡 The mixed-index error links to React's controlled-inputs page and misstates when the mode is fixed

**Location:** `packages/react/src/internals/composite/list/CompositeList.tsx` (register)

```ts
'More info: https://fb.me/react-controlled-components',
```

That shortlink targets React's controlled *form inputs* docs — unrelated to composite item indexes, and borrowed from the classic MUI form-state warning. The repo's error guidelines call for a Base UI docs link when applicable; here I'd drop the link entirely (or point at the Combobox `index` prop docs). Also, "The nature of the state is determined during the first render" isn't quite what the code does: it's determined by the **first registered item** and resets whenever the list empties. Worth rewording so the message matches the mechanism.

## Verdict

**Request changes** — the deep-subtree registration regression (Bugs No. 1) is confirmed by a failing repro against this branch that passes on master.

